  <article class="content-page section-space shell"><p class="eyebrow">Our reason to build.</p><h1 class="mt-5">A big dog.<br>A bigger reason.</h1><p class="mt-7 text-xl">When Buddy stopped using his hind legs, the search for support became personal. He is a very large dog. Few of the options we found came in a suitable size, and price added another hurdle.</p><p>We wanted more than a smaller bill. We wanted big dogs to be considered from the beginning, with equipment that could be adjusted, cared for and repaired.</p>
    <figure class="mt-10"><img src="/images/buddy-meadow.webp" width="1280" height="720" class="w-full rounded-xl" alt="Dog wheelchair design in a sunlit meadow"><figcaption class="mt-2 text-xs muted">Design visualization. Buddy's story is real; the pictured dog is not Buddy.</figcaption></figure>
    <h2>Make room for the gentle giants.</h2><p>Buddy changed where we started. Large-dog proportions, practical handling and a useful repair path are central to our wheelchair design. The smallest companions deserve that same attention.</p>
    <h2>Keep the good parts going.</h2><p>Metal rails. Standard running gear. Replaceable connections and washable soft support. We believe a worn part should have a repair path, and a well-loved frame should have more walks in it.</p>
    <h2>Some adventures call for a ride.</h2><p>Buddy Rover grows from the same idea. A long run, a slow walk by the sea, a familiar route in a different season. When a dog needs to ride, there should still be a place for them in the day.</p>
    <h2>More time together is the point.</h2><p>We cannot promise what any dog's recovery will look like. We can build around what matters to the people who love them: fit, comfort, repairability and the chance to keep sharing the outside.</p><div class="flex flex-wrap gap-4 mt-8"><a href="/shop" class="button-primary">Explore the wheelchairs</a><a href="/rover" class="button-secondary">Meet Buddy Rover</a></div>
